WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:03.480
In this lesson, we're going to take a look at

2
00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:05.240
Lumen Visualizers,

3
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.120
and we're also going to look at some tips and tricks

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.220
to get you going in the right direction

5
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:10.720
when it comes to Lumen.

6
00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:12.699
So let's get started.

7
00:00:12.699 --> 00:00:15.080
The Lumen Visualizers allow us to actually see

8
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.960
what's being done and being calculated under the hood.

9
00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.719
You also have under show,

10
00:00:21.719 --> 00:00:23.440
the ability to turn off things

11
00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:26.780
that are being calculated one by one,

12
00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:32.039
not as a whole, as you would with the Lumen Visualizers.

13
00:00:32.119 --> 00:00:34.420
The Lumen Visualizers give you an overview

14
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:37.659
of your old scene, your reflection, and your surface catch.

15
00:00:37.659 --> 00:00:39.659
But under show, under Lumen,

16
00:00:39.659 --> 00:00:41.859
we can actually pick walk through all the things

17
00:00:41.859 --> 00:00:44.259
being calculated on a more distinct level

18
00:00:44.259 --> 00:00:45.840
and turn them off.

19
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:49.179
Let's go and take a look at this briefly in Unreal

20
00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:51.729
and to see how to access these.

21
00:00:51.729 --> 00:00:54.429
To do so, we're going to go underneath lit,

22
00:00:54.429 --> 00:00:56.710
first off, and go to Lumen

23
00:00:56.869 --> 00:00:58.469
and look at the Lumen overview.

24
00:00:58.469 --> 00:01:01.600
Again, these are the Lumen visualize mode.

25
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.020
I'll click on overview.

26
00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.219
This is going to show me my geometry normals,

27
00:01:06.219 --> 00:01:09.260
my hardware ray tracing hit lighting,

28
00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:10.799
and my Lumen scene,

29
00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.180
and it's showing pink missing surface catch coverage,

30
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.180
yellow called surface catch.

31
00:01:17.180 --> 00:01:20.819
So basically it's giving me kind of my overall scene look

32
00:01:20.819 --> 00:01:22.319
at what's being called

33
00:01:22.319 --> 00:01:26.319
and what's being calculated to my liking.

34
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.939
Now, if you see, if you move away,

35
00:01:27.939 --> 00:01:31.819
you'll see some yellow and some even pink in the distance

36
00:01:31.819 --> 00:01:33.939
based on our angle and positioning.

37
00:01:33.939 --> 00:01:36.700
As we get closer, you'll see some of those go away.

38
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.140
Now, some of these pieces,

39
00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.980
when they're all one piece welded,

40
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.060
you will see some of those pink areas

41
00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:46.620
for Unreal is trying to calculate them as best they can.

42
00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.260
What you really want to avoid more than anything else

43
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:50.719
are black areas.

44
00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:53.340
And that brings us to our next topic.

45
00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:55.299
To be able to see those black areas,

46
00:01:55.319 --> 00:02:00.319
we can use a Unreal visualize card placement number one

47
00:02:00.319 --> 00:02:01.840
in our C-vars.

48
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:03.280
Now what this does overall,

49
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.549
what is happening is Lumen generates an automatic,

50
00:02:07.549 --> 00:02:09.550
now it's a big word here,

51
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:14.009
para-materialization of the nearby scene surface

52
00:02:14.009 --> 00:02:16.400
called a surface catch.

53
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.520
Used to quickly look up lighting

54
00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:21.439
at ray hit points in the scene.

55
00:02:21.439 --> 00:02:24.439
Lumen captures the material properties for each mesh

56
00:02:24.460 --> 00:02:27.099
from multiple angles called cards

57
00:02:27.099 --> 00:02:30.699
and are generated offline for each mesh.

58
00:02:30.699 --> 00:02:34.289
So these cards can be seen and visualized ahead of time.

59
00:02:34.289 --> 00:02:36.490
Now, if you get black areas on your mesh,

60
00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:38.490
as you do this visualization,

61
00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:40.210
that is telling you that those black areas

62
00:02:40.210 --> 00:02:43.689
aren't being calculated correctly and there's an issue.

63
00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:45.610
You'll see two different pieces here.

64
00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:48.530
Here's one, which is a little bit more open

65
00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.800
and may even be welded in this particular case.

66
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:55.460
But in this one, it's all one contained unit

67
00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:59.860
to be able to walk through and have people interact with.

68
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:02.259
Unfortunately, Lumen doesn't really like that so much

69
00:03:02.259 --> 00:03:04.500
because it's having a hard time calculating

70
00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:07.500
where surfaces begin and end.

71
00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.539
So it's better to be able to build your stuff

72
00:03:09.539 --> 00:03:12.340
out of best practice on an individual basis

73
00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.139
where you have a wall, make it very modulated,

74
00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:18.159
build it like as if you're building a Lego set,

75
00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:19.819
or you can think of it as in the real world,

76
00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.219
how a house is actually built.

77
00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:23.939
Now, a lot of times that that's a little bit better

78
00:03:23.939 --> 00:03:26.419
and even in production workflows, that's better

79
00:03:26.419 --> 00:03:29.879
because then you can customize each piece.

80
00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:31.919
If you're using Houdini, you can use,

81
00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:34.060
or even our geometry nodes,

82
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:37.259
or I should say procedural generated nodes.

83
00:03:37.259 --> 00:03:39.860
You can actually use those in either case,

84
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:42.620
whether it's Houdini geometry nodes or in Unreal,

85
00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:45.099
and you can build things on a practical basis

86
00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:48.710
where it's individual pieces and change them on the fly.

87
00:03:48.729 --> 00:03:50.530
So that's really a better method

88
00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:52.889
when it comes to looking at things in Unreal.

89
00:03:52.889 --> 00:03:54.330
Let's go into Unreal

90
00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:57.870
and actually apply this visualization card placement

91
00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:00.729
and take a look at how things are being calculated

92
00:04:00.729 --> 00:04:02.780
on this dynamic level.

93
00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:05.780
In Unreal, I'm gonna go to my output lock.

94
00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.159
I'm gonna cheat a little bit.

95
00:04:07.159 --> 00:04:11.800
I already have the visual card placement CVAR

96
00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.560
already active and ready to go.

97
00:04:13.560 --> 00:04:16.800
I'm gonna hit my, let me get rid of this one here.

98
00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.279
I'm gonna hit my up arrow

99
00:04:18.779 --> 00:04:22.800
and we'll see we have a Lumen visualize card placement at one.

100
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.220
Before we do that though,

101
00:04:24.220 --> 00:04:27.379
let me make sure I put everything back to regular lit mode

102
00:04:27.379 --> 00:04:30.620
just for safe precautionary measures there.

103
00:04:30.620 --> 00:04:33.339
And I'm gonna go ahead and switch that card placement

104
00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:36.579
to a one and hit enter.

105
00:04:36.579 --> 00:04:38.139
Now Unreal is going to show me

106
00:04:38.139 --> 00:04:40.459
how it is calculating these objects

107
00:04:40.459 --> 00:04:43.860
and their surface information all at one time.

108
00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.680
It seems to be doing a pretty good job.

109
00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.899
This is built very modularly.

110
00:04:48.439 --> 00:04:50.279
You'll see that there's pieces connected

111
00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:52.160
and pushed into each other

112
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:54.379
as if it was built in the real world.

113
00:04:54.379 --> 00:04:56.480
And I'm gonna go ahead and turn that off.

114
00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.779
I'm gonna hit my up arrow, set that to zero

115
00:04:59.779 --> 00:05:00.620
and hit enter.

116
00:05:00.620 --> 00:05:03.410
And now we can see that it is turned off.

117
00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:06.370
So the surface catch can be used to see areas

118
00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.769
that don't have surface catch coverage.

119
00:05:08.769 --> 00:05:12.250
They're colored pink in the surface catch view mode

120
00:05:12.250 --> 00:05:13.529
of the level editor.

121
00:05:13.529 --> 00:05:17.529
And you'll see these areas are in pink.

122
00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.269
You'll notice so these areas will not bounce light

123
00:05:20.269 --> 00:05:22.910
and will appear black in reflections.

124
00:05:22.910 --> 00:05:24.550
So you gotta be careful with that.

125
00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.949
So to be able to find those black areas

126
00:05:26.949 --> 00:05:29.589
to make sure they do not become a problem,

127
00:05:29.589 --> 00:05:33.110
you can use your visualizer to maybe spot them,

128
00:05:33.110 --> 00:05:34.709
but also if you need to,

129
00:05:34.709 --> 00:05:39.680
you can also increase the amount of mesh cards per task.

130
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.879
Now, again, the best practice as you see a note down here

131
00:05:43.879 --> 00:05:47.800
is to separate your pieces as much as possible

132
00:05:48.639 --> 00:05:50.620
and even modulate them because obviously

133
00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.439
that'll make it a little bit less on the draw calls.

134
00:05:53.439 --> 00:05:54.620
Does that mean you can't merge?

135
00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:55.939
No, you can merge pieces,

136
00:05:55.939 --> 00:05:58.819
but be aware of the issues that may occur.

137
00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:01.899
So just be very smart and deterministic when you do so.

138
00:06:01.899 --> 00:06:04.600
Only merge as needed, but at the same time,

139
00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:07.899
there's also the ability to merge Nanite objects,

140
00:06:07.899 --> 00:06:12.600
which we get into other classes using the merge tool.

141
00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:14.959
Let's take a look here at some tips and tricks.

142
00:06:14.959 --> 00:06:17.720
So Lumen reflections support more than one bounce

143
00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:21.259
when hardware ray tracing is enabled with hit lighting.

144
00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:23.420
So remember, it actually can help out a lot

145
00:06:23.420 --> 00:06:25.220
if your card is able to do it.

146
00:06:25.220 --> 00:06:28.060
And the max reflection bounces setting

147
00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.819
in the post process file is set to two or greater.

148
00:06:30.819 --> 00:06:31.939
So keep those things in mind

149
00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:34.459
when you're actually using this particular feature.

150
00:06:34.459 --> 00:06:36.680
This can prevent black areas and reflections

151
00:06:36.680 --> 00:06:39.980
when there is enough performance budget to allow it.

152
00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.939
So again, having a good card to be able to support this

153
00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:44.980
is gonna be really key.

154
00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.959
Lumen reflections can be used without Lumen GI,

155
00:06:47.959 --> 00:06:50.100
so you can turn those on if you needed

156
00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.160
for games and applications which use static lighting.

157
00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.399
So just be very deterministic.

158
00:06:55.399 --> 00:06:57.240
I always say, remember where you parked.

159
00:06:57.240 --> 00:06:58.720
That means remember your settings.

160
00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:02.000
So if you do mix and match your GI and your reflection,

161
00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:04.279
say maybe you're using ray trace reflections,

162
00:07:04.279 --> 00:07:07.360
you're using Lumen GI, you're using some other GI

163
00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.560
and you're using Lumen reflections,

164
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.019
just remember where those settings lie

165
00:07:12.019 --> 00:07:13.720
and what you have set in

166
00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.019
so that you can troubleshoot things ahead of time.

167
00:07:16.019 --> 00:07:18.639
So just be careful with that.

168
00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:22.180
So Lumen hardware ray tracing optimizations,

169
00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:26.160
you'll see including enabling async compute

170
00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.240
for hardware ray tracing by default on console.

171
00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:31.639
So this is in there for you to actually check things

172
00:07:31.639 --> 00:07:34.199
and to make sure things are working correctly

173
00:07:34.199 --> 00:07:36.680
when it comes to producing things for console.

174
00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:39.579
Lumen overview visualization mode,

175
00:07:39.579 --> 00:07:42.360
you'll see in 5.3 and includes the view of geometry

176
00:07:42.379 --> 00:07:44.540
which we saw normals of the Lumen scene,

177
00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:48.019
make it easier to spot issues with global distance fields

178
00:07:48.019 --> 00:07:49.980
in the case of software ray tracing

179
00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:51.579
or Nanite fallback meshes

180
00:07:51.579 --> 00:07:53.819
in the case of hardware ray tracing.

181
00:07:53.819 --> 00:07:56.819
So we've added that and it's very helpful for you.

182
00:07:56.819 --> 00:07:58.060
You'll also see a CVAR here.

183
00:07:58.060 --> 00:08:00.500
It says Lumen reflections and visualize traces

184
00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.699
and is now added for visualizing

185
00:08:02.699 --> 00:08:04.639
and debugging reflection rays.

186
00:08:04.639 --> 00:08:06.459
So you can also use that CVAR too.

187
00:08:07.399 --> 00:08:10.199
In 5.3 material AO updated,

188
00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:13.379
we've now updated it so that previously

189
00:08:13.379 --> 00:08:16.139
it was applied on top of the emissive and indirect lighting

190
00:08:16.139 --> 00:08:18.620
but now you'll actually get a better result.

191
00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:20.459
And you have to make sure your static

192
00:08:20.459 --> 00:08:22.040
is lighting is turned off,

193
00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:24.060
which we talked about previously,

194
00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.790
because Lumen as you see at the very bottom

195
00:08:27.790 --> 00:08:30.529
does not support static lighting.

196
00:08:30.529 --> 00:08:32.809
Quick review for softening up those shadows.

197
00:08:32.809 --> 00:08:35.889
We have source angle on a directional light

198
00:08:35.889 --> 00:08:38.350
will soften your shadows,

199
00:08:38.350 --> 00:08:40.830
your source radius on a local light

200
00:08:40.850 --> 00:08:43.840
will soften your shadows.

201
00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:46.279
So reminder about ray tracing mode.

202
00:08:46.279 --> 00:08:48.960
This is going to be very beneficial for you

203
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.480
but you can choose this,

204
00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:55.220
whatever your card can support.

205
00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:57.139
If you're going to use a detailed tracing

206
00:08:57.139 --> 00:08:58.220
or global tracing,

207
00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.899
just be very deterministic when using it.

208
00:09:00.899 --> 00:09:04.139
So that's about it with this one.

209
00:09:04.139 --> 00:09:07.799
Here is some links to help you further your knowledge

210
00:09:07.799 --> 00:09:09.750
on these topics.

211
00:09:09.750 --> 00:09:10.789
And in the next video,

212
00:09:10.809 --> 00:09:12.529
we're going to take a look at Nanite

213
00:09:12.529 --> 00:09:15.580
in virtual shadow maps and how they work.

214
